<section
  id="case-studiobid-logotype"
  class="fullscreen-section case-study-section"
  data-title="Case Study: StudioBid — Brand &amp; Logotype | Katja Turnsek"
  data-description="Graphic design case study for a clear, product-ready auction identity with flexible logo variations and UI applications."
  data-case="studiobid"
  aria-labelledby="case-studiobid-logotype-title"
>
  <div class="rows">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-header">
        <p class="case-eyebrow">Graphic Design</p>
        <h1 id="case-studiobid-logotype-title">StudioBid</h1>
        <p class="case-subtitle">A direct, UI-ready auction identity designed
          for clarity, recognition, and flexible product use.</p>

        <dl class="case-summary" aria-label="Project summary">
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Brand identity and logo design</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual Semester Project 2</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Adobe Illustrator, Figma</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>UI-ready logo system</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class="tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper">
          <div
            class="demo-links"
            data-auto="demo"
            data-case="studiobid-logotype"
          ></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class="project-switcher"
          aria-label="StudioBid sections"
          data-auto="switcher"
          data-case="studiobid"
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">The brief</h2>
      <p>
        StudioBid needed a modern identity that communicated “auction” quickly
        and felt trustworthy inside a digital product. The logo had to work in a
        narrow navigation area, scale down to an icon, and stay strong enough
        for presentations and promotional layouts without becoming visually
        heavy.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="case-study-grid">
        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--challenge">
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            A gavel is immediately understood, but it is also a familiar auction
            symbol. The challenge was to keep that useful recognition while
            giving StudioBid its own visual rhythm and ensuring the mark
            remained legible at product-interface sizes.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Communicate the platform purpose without explanation</li>
            <li>Differentiate a familiar symbol through proportion and colour</li>
            <li>Create variations for small, wide, light, and dark placements</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--explore">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-studiobid-logosuite-600.webp"
            data-src="design-studiobid-logosuite-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="391"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="StudioBid logo suite showing primary and secondary wordmarks, gavel icons, monochrome versions, and reversed applications"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Logo suite built for navigation,
            icons, and contrasting backgrounds</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class="case-study-block case-study-block--decisions">
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Reduced the gavel to a bold silhouette so it remains recognisable
              when used alone at icon and favicon sizes
            </li>
            <li>
              Highlighted “Bid” in vivid pink to emphasise the platform's core
              action and give the wordmark a memorable endpoint
            </li>
            <li>
              Used a heavy, geometric wordmark to balance the symbol and create
              a confident product identity
            </li>
            <li>
              Prepared primary, secondary, icon, monochrome, and reversed
              versions before testing the mark within the actual interface
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class="case-study-item case-study-media--booking">
          <img
            class="thumb"
            src="{{base}}assets/images/design-studiobid-600.webp"
            data-src="design-studiobid-600.webp"
            data-path="assets/images"
            width="600"
            height="434"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            alt="StudioBid gavel icon and wordmark displayed at large size on a white background"
          />
          <figcaption class="case-caption">Primary mark combining the gavel
            silhouette with the action-led wordmark</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row case-outcome">
      <h2 class="case-section-heading">Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The resulting identity is deliberately direct: users can recognise the
        auction context quickly, while the pink accent and compact lockups give
        StudioBid a consistent product presence. Designing the logo alongside
        the interface helped me evaluate it as a working UI component rather
        than only as a presentation graphic.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="back-to-work-container">
      <a
        href="{{base}}#work"
        class="label-link back-to-work-label"
